;;;; net/bare-perf.lisp -- a sampling profiler for bare-metal AArch64 (the Pi images).
;;;;
;;;; The counting half is lib/a64-pmu.lisp, baked into the same image: (pmu-measure thunk ...).
;;;; This is the sampling half, made of a few hundred bytes of native code that this file writes
;;;; into an exec page at run time (the hdmi-console / kiln blit way), so nothing in the
;;;; translator moves:
;;;;
;;;;   SAMPLING.  (perf-start :hz 1000) arms the EL2 hypervisor timer (CNTHP) on each core and
;;;;   routes it to FIQ through the BCM2836 local controller (0x40000040 + 4*core, bit 6) -- from
;;;;   the native arm stub: a Lisp :u32 MEM-REF of that register took a synchronous external
;;;;   abort on QEMU (the device takes 32-bit accesses only), where the stub's LDR W does not.  The
;;;;   FIQ vector (current EL, SPx: VBAR_EL2 + 0x300, a bare `B .' until now) jumps to a native
;;;;   handler that re-arms the timer and appends one record to the core's ring: the PC, the
;;;;   interrupted x30, and up to DEPTH return addresses from the frame-record chain (every modus
;;;;   frame has one at x29, f5a9e0b).  The walk is bounded (8-aligned -- the board's stacks are
;;;;   not 16-aligned, so x29 ends in 8 -- inside [lo, hi), strictly
;;;;   upward), so a broken chain ends the record and never faults.  The handler touches no Lisp
;;;;   heap, takes no lock, and allocates nothing.  (perf-stop) disarms; (perf-report) folds the
;;;;   rings into "count pc;caller;caller..." lines for a host to symbolize.
;;;;
;;;;   WHY FIQ AND THE HYPERVISOR TIMER.  The virtual timer is already routed to IRQ on core 0 so
;;;;   that WFI wakes in the network's idle delay (net/arch-raspi3b.lisp) -- with IRQs left masked
;;;;   on purpose.  A sampler on that timer, or one that unmasked IRQs, would change when the idle
;;;;   delay returns.  CNTHP and FIQ are used by nothing else here.
;;;;
;;;; The assembly is net/bare-perf-fiq.s; the vector below is what
;;;; llvm-mc assembles it to (net/bare-perf-gen.py: `llvm-mc -triple=aarch64', the .text bytes).
;;;; Change the .s, not the numbers.
;;;;
;;;; RAW WORDS.  The handler's fields are raw machine words, and a :u64 MEM-REF hands a raw word
;;;; back AS A TAGGED VALUE -- so every raw field is read and written as two :u32 halves.

;;; ---- GENERATED by net/bare-perf-gen.py from bare-perf-fiq.s: edit that ----
(defparameter *perf-fiq-words*
